{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/966373-1e-what-was-playing-vtr-like-in-its-early-days","id":966373,"title":"(1E) What was playing VTR like in its early days?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-09-09T12:18:20-07:00","id":966373,"title":"(1E) What was playing VTR like in its early days?","content":"I often see threads and discussions on various websites asking what Vampire: The Masquerade was like when it first came out and how people played the game back in 1991 and 1992. It's kind of obvious as to why, VTM was a major turning point in RPG history and the early 1990's was over two decades ago. Plus oWoD ended up radically different from how it started in many different ways.<br /> <br /> \u200bBut what about Requiem? Now that the game is in its second edition and has also been changed significantly, along with the rest of the New World of Darkness (which is now called Chronicles of Darkness, though for sake of simplicity, I will refer to 1e material as nWoD and 2e material as CofD), I want to spark discussion on the early days of Requiem and the New World of Darkness.<br /> <br /> I know 2004 was more recent than 1991, but it's been over a decade ago now, and I didn't get into World of Darkness until 2010, when I was in high school (I was born in 1993). By the time I started playing WoD, the First Edition of nWoD had more or less completed its run, barring a few minor PDF updates here and there. I want to know what this early period in the gameline's history was like, with an emphasis on 2004-2006 (pre-Changeling, which was when the nWoD fully discovered its own identity before forming a new one as the Chronicles of Darkness later on).<br /> <br /> I was on the old forums from 2010-2012, and it was different from what it is now, but I'd imagine things were even more wild back then. I want to know what the speculation on nWoD was like following the demise of oWoD, and what people thought of Requiem. I'm talking about those who actually played it of course, and not those who dismissed it and just stuck to Masquerade. I know a lot of people did that, including most of the gamers in my area, which is why I played Masquerade for so long, because nobody but me wanted to play Requiem. Nothing wrong with preferring Masquerade over Requiem or vice versa, I like both equally, but I'm seeking info from long-time Requiem fans.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1579-camilla","name":"Camilla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1579_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1044"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-09T13:02:25-07:00","id":966403,"title":"","content":"First and foremost, 1E Requiem seemed to fix a lot of the things that rubbed me the wrong way about oWoD. The switch to Blood Potency from Generation alone I feel would have made the switch worth it. I loved the Fog of Eternity (sad to see it mostly go in 2E) so that there was no way to prove what creation myth was real, actual reasons for not everyone to be playing some famous historical figure (maybe they thought they were, but odds are on fevered torpor dreams and they were just some rando.)<br /> <br /> I really enjoy the culling down to five basic clans based on fictional archetypes, then the broadening to Bloodlines to represent regional, cultural or philosophical differences. I do remember being sad that my favorite Clan had Presence replaced with Animalism, but I came around to the theme. <br /> <br /> Breaking away from the metaplot was also good for stuff I liked. Giving each setting/city its own focus as opposed to them just being foot-notes on the Grand Sabbat/Cam/Anarch war appealed. The Covenants as factions that worked against each other more cold-war like than the all-out ones from Masquerade was another in that vein.<br /> <br /> There wasn't as much as a narrative shift like 2E has, but the seeds were obviously planted which was great for where I wanted to go from oWoD. I did LARP as well, and there were real discussions about whether to switch over or not. I had no interest in a game that was going to stay Masq, but gave the Requiem one a shot. <br /> <br /> I was fascinated by the Ordo at first, but then came to settle with the Invictus as my home-base Covenant.<br /> <br /> I like the kind of separation they made, keeping each line kind of it's own strain, with the assumption that crossovers would be rare or just very customized, so in a Vampire game, the Vampires had the numbers, etc.<br /> <br /> I have mixed feelings about Paradox and 5E. On one hand, I'm glad to see the IP actually doing stuff again. On the other hand, going back to Masquerade from Requiem feels like a retrograde on some level, so we'll see.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4047-holy","name":"Holy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4047_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"274"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-09T13:08:27-07:00","id":966405,"title":"","content":"I agree a lot about Requiem 1E fixing a lot of the issues Revised Masquerade seemed to have, especially the broadening of the clans into basic archetypes. The only thing I would fix about Requiem 1E is removing the &quot;emotional deadness&quot; that the fluff in the 1E corebook sort of pushed, especially since there's no mechanical enforcement for it.<br /> <br /> Also, I am sad to see the Fog of Ages gone as well. I have a lot of greivances with 2E/CofD, but that's best suited for another thread.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1579-camilla","name":"Camilla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1579_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1044"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-09T13:50:20-07:00","id":966418,"title":"","content":"As someone who came in when nWoD started it was mostly good, with just a few odditys here and there. We had a ongoing debate about Humanity loss from frenzy and a strength 1 charter was better off getting a heavy pistol over a light one because you only ever took a -1 penalty regardless of how much you fell short of the required stats to use it and we had so many ways to cheese the system (infinight feeding on other kindred using the Coils anyone?). As I don't have any contex for cWoD beyond what others said about it anything you would like me to go into more depth on?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4714-live-bait","name":"Live Bait","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"703"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-09T18:29:24-07:00","id":966514,"title":"","content":"For Revised Masquerade when I came in, it felt almost like a war/roleplaying game and it was easy to become consumed by the power creep which may have been because of my experience with D&amp;D 3e and it heading in the same direction at the time. I think the biggest shift with Requiem is I understood the intention of the Storyteller system and it was not just a different set of rules. Requiem had enough ties to Masquerade to make the transition easy. With 2e the game has shaken off its ties to Masquerade and become its own thing completely. It is no longer trying to not be Masquerade which is nice because there was some good things there. I look at 2e as the spiritual successor to Masquerade and 1e as the transition between the two. I'm not sure I actually was on the boards immediately. When I did come on I remember lots of confusion about the rules. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs","name":"Diggs","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-09T18:51:13-07:00","id":966524,"title":"","content":"I started with Requiem shortly after it came out, having never really played any old World of Darkness. I only joined the online community fairly recently. 2013, if my join date wasn't shifted by the site change.<br /> <br /> I found the pack of a metaplot refreshing, but some design decisions were strange, such as the frenzy upon encountering vampires. It's the kind of thing that could wipe out a party if you introduce them to the wrong antagonist and suffered some poor dice rolls. The Fog of Eternity, too, seemed sort of restraining.<br /> <br /> A problem for me that only really surfaced later was the atmosphere. While new World of Darkness was meant to be its own system where the darkness was about mystery and Gothic Punk wasn't a 'thing,' the Requiem book still clung to a lot of Masquerade's conceits, where the World of Darkness is dark because more people are evil. P202 says, 'Exaggerate the negative in a real-world city to turn it into a hellhole,' in order to fit your location into the World of Darkness.<br /> <br /> While I found the new bloodlines and the lack of internalism in vampire society a great advantage, so that a covenant was not the monolithic entity a clan could be in Masquerade, there weren't a lot of examples. The fiction trilogy that started things off was good, but it really only portrayed the Invictus and Carthians and a bit of the Lancea et Sanctum. The books could be a slog to read through, too, without examples or fiction to break up the monotonous drone of lecturing text. I've found the fiction of old World of Darkness really indicates a lot of things for people, though maybe I'm wrong, and the lack of it for new World of Darkness, as well as clinging artifacts from the past, really seemed to suggest you should just bring personalities, attitudes and such over. But then there wasn't too much support for that, either.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 09-09-2016, 09:53 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-09T20:26:31-07:00","id":966558,"title":"","content":"I like to think of classic wod as a massive wiki edit war. Now that being a bold statement. It was good for its time and really broke ground. and I've had many enjoyable games with it.<br /> <br /> The first few ideas and books of maqs were small time and a lot of the focus was more local and you can understand motives and direction. It created a very solid set world mythology. That english readers could grasp. Then you got small one off ideas to explore more narrative space, but because of the previous set in stone narrative, you got a lot of chaffing and unwieldiness to open new ideas. I'm pointing to the Generation Stat. How can any other ideologically / presentable narrative be right when you make it such a part of the stat/mechanic block. There was no well maybe I'm wrong? There was, No you are wrong and you're getting slapped for not knowing.<br /> <br /> Right when the Sabbot/Cam War became a thing into the thing it really just consumed the world and all the stories into this awkward cross country war plot. It was bad because it made good vampires by difference. Can you really say you're the bad guys when the other side murders families like its nothing? and when you've pulled the pin out of the grenade there was no going back on that ethical question. and made all the small plots irrelevant and silly. How can we argue and fight about feeding grounds when the barbarians are at the gates?<br /> <br /> These two things really pulled the game from a focus of damnation and unliving to the heights of gonzo, its famous for.<br /> <br /> And don't get me started on the publishing new powers to counter the previous powers you accidentally made. Requiem felt like a fresh start and a return to the beginnings of Masqu ideas. And it actually took a bunch of the really cool and interesting idea from oldworld and really let them flow. Look at the Sactum and the Circle, those ideas of monsters for chirst and monster we are. are taken directly from the sabbot, and because it's not tied to a war zone narrative, you can highlight good point and bad points. no one is the designated good guy or bad guy. I remember reading the Lasombra book and thinking, monsters for chirst. thats an interesting idea and could be played around with. And pagan vampires is kind of a trope all on it's own.<br /> <br /> Because you see the sabbot had this very weird thing about it. It had more freedom to do what it wanted in terms of narrative space. So really you had like two different sabbots. The higher up ideals and the common murder hobos. and marring these factions together instantly made them the wrong bad ideas when really they were just different viewpoints to the common problem. But because of the narrative stone from before. you couldn't change the setting's narrative.<br /> <br /> And then you had the massive wiki edits as I like to call them. each new writer had to have their own little spin on their pet clan/bloodline/sect. which felt like a big No you're wrong, because you don't have these books. I really liked a lot of the fiction that got run, the historical and family line plots were interesting and pretty good. but the meta plot just killed it.<br /> <br /> With the nwod's focus on the local city, you can have each city run a little differently and play with the different themes. And it doesn't destroy the verisimilitude of the setting.<br /> <br /> The biggest sell I've made for Nwod over old is simple. You can play old world style games in New Wod. But you can't play New wod in Old world. You still run sealed evil in the can. master manipulators. and good guy fighting the greater darkness. The one thing I will bring up is that; in an attempt to not gonzo the nwod game they made the splat a notch too weak. They really scaled the power back a bit much. But I know what they were going for. which is why I'm happy about 2ed really taking another crack at it.<br /> <br /> Fog of ages was interesting. but I never played too much with it. but I understood what they were going for. You can't remember everything you done for forever. It's too much of a headache. but you definitely remember the important stuff. Blood Potency was a god sent. It massively decoupled the previous meta being completely right.<br /> <br /> oldwod edition to edition are really just more of the same. I never felt like the mechanics really saw much of a difference. More the same, with a world update for tech sake.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Placeholder","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11731-mercset","name":"MercSet","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11731_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"277"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-11T03:48:44-07:00","id":967254,"title":"","content":"I agree with the above, especially the part about the 'power levels' being different. You felt incredibly fragile as a vampire in nWoD 1e (e.g. a small group of mortals could beat the crap out of a centuries old vampire). This has been rectified in Chronicles of Darkness where vampires have returned to some semblance of being a real threat to mortals once again - especially in the context of a Hunters game.<br /> <br /> The big positive I liked most about Requiem when it was first introduced was the sense of locality. It was less focused on any worldwide struggle between two monolithic entities: the Sabs vs the Cammie. It tried to keep things focused on the characters and the play-space was scaled way back.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1592-jack","name":"Jack","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1592_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"329"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-11T05:15:55-07:00","id":967272,"title":"","content":"1e's fluff gave me the feeling that pretty much every city was off on its own, a beacon of relative safety in the wilderness for both humans and vampires, but vampires especially were cut off from all contact with other cities; the idea of traveling to another city seemed like a major undertaking. 2e has scaled back on this theme of urban isolation: cloistered domains are still possible, but they aren't necessarily the rule, and you get the sense that vampires who have some level of regional community or organization. <br /> <br /> The Fog of Ages was a creepy aspect of the game, and it was definitely interesting, but I wasn't personally a fan of it. To me, it seemed like it was the writers' way of explaining why the setting was so vague: &quot;Why don't vampires have a perfect record of their history? Because they get amnesia or have their memories scrambled when they are torpid!&quot; There are other ways to explain that stuff away, like conspiracy, or destruction of records, or death of witnesses, or stuff like that. That said, I like the idea of the Fog of Ages being a consequence of, say, going into torpor due to violence, and then it messes with memories in relation to the event that sent them into torpor: the further away from the incident, the easier it is to jog your memories; the closer to the event, the harder it is, and you may never get those memories back.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1122743-haberdasher-s-requiem-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#B22222\">Haberdasher's Requiem Conversions and Homebrew</span></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6498-haberdasher","name":"Haberdasher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6498_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"818"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-11T07:43:13-07:00","id":967302,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Haberdasher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/966373-1e-what-was-playing-vtr-like-in-its-early-days?p=967272#post967272\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1e's fluff gave me the feeling that pretty much every city was off on its own, a beacon of relative safety in the wilderness for both humans and vampires, but vampires especially were cut off from all contact with other cities; the idea of traveling to another city seemed like a major undertaking. 2e has scaled back on this theme of urban isolation: cloistered domains are still possible, but they aren't necessarily the rule, and you get the sense that vampires who have some level of regional community or organization. <br /> <br /> The Fog of Ages was a creepy aspect of the game, and it was definitely interesting, but I wasn't personally a fan of it. To me, it seemed like it was the writers' way of explaining why the setting was so vague: &amp;quot;Why don't vampires have a perfect record of their history? Because they get amnesia or have their memories scrambled when they are torpid!&amp;quot; There are other ways to explain that stuff away, like conspiracy, or destruction of records, or death of witnesses, or stuff like that. That said, I like the idea of the Fog of Ages being a consequence of, say, going into torpor due to violence, and then it messes with memories in relation to the event that sent them into torpor: the further away from the incident, the easier it is to jog your memories; the closer to the event, the harder it is, and you may never get those memories back.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think both of these were cases of overcorrecting in the aftermath of Masquerade. We don't want massive globe-spanning conspiracies this time, so we have to drive home that each city exists in complete isolation from each other city. We don't want a one true origin for vampires this time, so we have to make sure it's impossible for anyone to remember that far back. Etc. 2e was able to let go of some elements that existed more to differentiate Requiem from Masquerade than to serve Requiem <i>per se</i>. It's at the point know where it doesn't have to be defined by how it's like Masquerade or by how it's different from Masquerade. It's grown into completely its own setting that doesn't care what Masquerade thinks of it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-11T10:27:45-07:00","id":967394,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/966373-1e-what-was-playing-vtr-like-in-its-early-days?p=967302#post967302\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think both of these were cases of overcorrecting in the aftermath of Masquerade. We don't want massive globe-spanning conspiracies this time, so we have to drive home that each city exists in complete isolation from each other city. We don't want a one true origin for vampires this time, so we have to make sure it's impossible for anyone to remember that far back. Etc. 2e was able to let go of some elements that existed more to differentiate Requiem from Masquerade than to serve Requiem <i>per se</i>. It's at the point know where it doesn't have to be defined by how it's like Masquerade or by how it's different from Masquerade. It's grown into completely its own setting that doesn't care what Masquerade thinks of it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Requiem did get past the &quot;keep everything local&quot; mentality, though. <i>\u200bDanse Macabre </i>touched on the idea of Tier 3 organizations, and I'm not averse to the idea that, on some level, the covenants have an individual or group who guides them on a global scale.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1122743-haberdasher-s-requiem-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#B22222\">Haberdasher's Requiem Conversions and Homebrew</span></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6498-haberdasher","name":"Haberdasher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6498_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"818"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-11T10:36:28-07:00","id":967399,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Haberdasher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/966373-1e-what-was-playing-vtr-like-in-its-early-days?p=967394#post967394\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Requiem did get past the &amp;quot;keep everything local&amp;quot; mentality, though. <i>\u200bDanse Macabre </i>touched on the idea of Tier 3 organizations, and I'm not averse to the idea that, on some level, the covenants have an individual or group who guides them on a global scale.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, absolutely. I guess I shouldn't have divided it along 1e/2e lines, since a lot of the ways Requiem has grown happened during 1e's run. But I'm getting off-track here anyway. This thread is about the early days of 1e. Myself, I'm in the same boat as Camilla, I didn't start playing Requiem until 2010, so my knowledge of the early part of the game's lifespan is limited to what I've heard, and what I can surmise from the early books.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-11T11:53:37-07:00","id":967441,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/966373-1e-what-was-playing-vtr-like-in-its-early-days?p=967399#post967399\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Oh, absolutely. I guess I shouldn't have divided it along 1e/2e lines, since a lot of the ways Requiem has grown happened during 1e's run. But I'm getting off-track here anyway. This thread is about the early days of 1e. Myself, I'm in the same boat as Camilla, I didn't start playing Requiem until 2010, so my knowledge of the early part of the game's lifespan is limited to what I've heard, and what I can surmise from the early books.</div> </div> </div> </div>Same. I only got my hand on the 1e corebook in my sophomore or junior year of high school, which was about 2008/2009. The game had really grown by then, and if I remember correctly, the clan books were out by then, which I think is the point when Requiem really found its tone- not for nothing are those books recommended in the &quot;Inspirational Material&quot; section of the 2e corebook.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1122743-haberdasher-s-requiem-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#B22222\">Haberdasher's Requiem Conversions and Homebrew</span></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6498-haberdasher","name":"Haberdasher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6498_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"818"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-11T12:58:24-07:00","id":967474,"title":"","content":"I'd love to run a barebones basic Requiem 1e game using only the VTR 1e corebook and the nWoD 1e corebook, while generally leaving the supplements out of it and the other 1e gamelines present but off to the side (after all, VTR 1e does mention Lupines and Magi, but doesn't go too into detail about them). There would be some modifications though. Predator's Taint and the emotional deadness angle both would be gotten rid of, and I may de-emphasize Humanity but I would not be removing it entirely.<br /> <br /> Maybe I could do it as a play-by-post on here. I tossed the idea around before, but never got off the ground with it. I think it's time to change that and actually get off my ass and do something with this idea.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1579-camilla","name":"Camilla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1579_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1044"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-11T14:17:09-07:00","id":967520,"title":"","content":"To be honest, I think part of the reason it never got off the ground is probably the fact that most Requiem fans are on the 2e bandwagon, at least on these here boards. And if you're running with just the core and nothing else, you're cutting off one of the most compelling reasons to play 1e over 2e, the greater volume of supplementary material.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-11T14:58:33-07:00","id":967540,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MercSet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/966373-1e-what-was-playing-vtr-like-in-its-early-days?p=966558#post966558\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">With the nwod's focus on the local city, you can have each city run a little differently and play with the different themes. And it doesn't destroy the verisimilitude</div> </div> </div> </div>Exactly how I feel........except did you really have to bring out the big words. Who uses Verisimilitude? I feel bad now for not know this word, thanks a lot. :P<br /> <br /> I really like the clans being reduced to just 5 archtypes and the option left open to have the local setting focus on convenants or clans (if you wanted to be more like oWoD).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":8306,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8306-wissenschaft","user":"Wissenschaft","date":". 09-11-2016, 06:13 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8306-wissenschaft","name":"Wissenschaft","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"234"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-11T15:14:16-07:00","id":967551,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wissenschaft</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/966373-1e-what-was-playing-vtr-like-in-its-early-days?p=967540#post967540\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Exactly how I feel........except did you really have to bring out the big words. Who uses Verisimilitude? I feel bad now for not know this word, thanks a lot. :P</div> </div> </div> </div>Fair dues good sir. I just like using it as a technical term. Because using believability / plausibility and attaching that to a game of make believe monsters, is just hard to justify. Just slipping some academia into a rant.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Placeholder","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11731-mercset","name":"MercSet","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11731_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"277"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-11T15:35:44-07:00","id":967566,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Camilla</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/966373-1e-what-was-playing-vtr-like-in-its-early-days?p=967474#post967474\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd love to run a barebones basic Requiem 1e game using only the VTR 1e corebook and the nWoD 1e corebook, while generally leaving the supplements out of it and the other 1e gamelines present but off to the side</div> </div> </div> </div>Can I ask what you like about the original 1E core? I did play 1E early on when it first came out, it's a nice game, but I don't think it's anywhere near as well designed or interesting as 2E, so I'd be interested in hearing the opposing viewpoint.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3326-an-fhuiseog","name":"An Fhuiseog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3326_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"271"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-11T15:38:33-07:00","id":967568,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/966373-1e-what-was-playing-vtr-like-in-its-early-days?p=967520#post967520\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To be honest, I think part of the reason it never got off the ground is probably the fact that most Requiem fans are on the 2e bandwagon, at least on these here boards. And if you're running with just the core and nothing else, you're cutting off one of the most compelling reasons to play 1e over 2e, the greater volume of supplementary material.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fair point, I guess I never considered that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>An Fhuiseog</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/966373-1e-what-was-playing-vtr-like-in-its-early-days?p=967566#post967566\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Can I ask what you like about the original 1E core? I did play 1E early on when it first came out, it's a nice game, but I don't think it's anywhere near as well designed or interesting as 2E, so I'd be interested in hearing the opposing viewpoint.</div> </div> </div> </div> \u200bFor me, 2E isn't the Requiem that had enthralled me back in 2010. The changed mechanics such as Touchstones and reworked Disciplines are a huge turn-off for me, as was the emphasis on the Strix (I always hated the Strix and felt them to be a terrible antagonist) to the point that the Strix had replaced VII and Belial's Brood as the main antagonists of the game (Belial's Brood isn't even mentioned in 2e). A lot of the mystery and wonder that 1E had was gone, and something entirely different had took its place. Something that just didn't sit right with me.<br /> <br /> And that's without mentioning the God-Machine and the rest of the gamelines.<br /> <br /> If you like 2e, that's fine, but it's not for me. I'd rather stick with 1e. No Strix, No God-Machine, that's my motto. (Yes, I'm well aware that the Strix debuted late in 1e, but they weren't the main stars of the show until 2e)<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1579,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1579-camilla","user":"Camilla","date":". 09-11-2016, 06:43 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1579-camilla","name":"Camilla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1579_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1044"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-11T15:51:29-07:00","id":967575,"title":"","content":"Sorry I was unclear, what do you like about 1E in and of itself, not in relation to 2E? i.e. ignoring the existence of 2E.<br /> <br /> I found 1E was going in an interesting direction in terms of what vampires were metaphysically and painted the outlines of an interesting vampire society, but that it was a bit too vague on details. I found it hard to know what a 1E vampire was or how they related to the world and their society.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3326-an-fhuiseog","name":"An Fhuiseog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3326_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"271"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-11T16:46:51-07:00","id":967601,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Camilla</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/966373-1e-what-was-playing-vtr-like-in-its-early-days?p=967568#post967568\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Fair point, I guess I never considered that.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> \u200bFor me, 2E isn't the Requiem that had enthralled me back in 2010. The changed mechanics such as Touchstones and reworked Disciplines are a huge turn-off for me, as was the emphasis on the Strix (I always hated the Strix and felt them to be a terrible antagonist) to the point that the Strix had replaced VII and Belial's Brood as the main antagonists of the game (Belial's Brood isn't even mentioned in 2e). A lot of the mystery and wonder that 1E had was gone, and something entirely different had took its place. Something that just didn't sit right with me.<br /> <br /> And that's without mentioning the God-Machine and the rest of the gamelines.<br /> <br /> If you like 2e, that's fine, but it's not for me. I'd rather stick with 1e. No Strix, No God-Machine, that's my motto. (Yes, I'm well aware that the Strix debuted late in 1e, but they weren't the main stars of the show until 2e)</div> </div> </div> </div>To be fair, the 1e core also had next to nothing on VII and Belial's Brood, and 2e can be played completely without the Strix. The God-Machine doesn't even have anything to do with Requiem unless you want it to, regardless of edition. Like, if you don't care for the changes to Disciplines (personally I found them all to be pretty minor changes, but YMMV) or other mechanical changes like the addition of Touchstones, that's a perfectly valid opinion. But, I don't think the Requiem in the 1e core book is the Requiem that enthralled you in 2010 any more than the 2e core book is. The Requiem that enthralled you in 2010 had no less than 33 published supplements. Possibly more, depending on how late in 2010 you discovered it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-11T16:54:12-07:00","id":967605,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/966373-1e-what-was-playing-vtr-like-in-its-early-days?p=967601#post967601\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To be fair, the 1e core also had next to nothing on VII and Belial's Brood, and 2e can be played completely without the Strix. The God-Machine doesn't even have anything to do with Requiem unless you want it to, regardless of edition. Like, if you don't care for the changes to Disciplines (personally I found them all to be pretty minor changes, but YMMV) or other mechanical changes like the addition of Touchstones, that's a perfectly valid opinion. But, I don't think the Requiem in the 1e core book is the Requiem that enthralled you in 2010 any more than the 2e core book is. The Requiem that enthralled you in 2010 had no less than 33 published supplements. Possibly more, depending on how late in 2010 you discovered it.</div> </div> </div> </div> \u200bI discovered it in early 2010 around January or February, with no prior knowledge of the supplements. Mainly I don't like the mechanical changes to 2e, since as you said, the Strix are supposedly optional and the God-Machine is more of a general 2e thing than anything specific to Requiem.<br /> <br /> As for the other poster's questions, one of the things I liked about 1e Requiem was that it was so vague, especially if all you had for a frame of reference was the corebooks (and for a very long time, that's all I did have). You could make it your own game, it was a toolkit and a sandbox that you could control and were encouraged to do so, unlike the metaplot-driven Masquerade. 2e seems to lack that sort of magic to it, at least for me. And of course, there are the mechanical changes like Touchstones, which is probably the biggest issue I have with 2e.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1579-camilla","name":"Camilla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1579_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1044"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-11T17:54:37-07:00","id":967630,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Camilla</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/966373-1e-what-was-playing-vtr-like-in-its-early-days?p=967605#post967605\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As for the other poster's questions, one of the things I liked about 1e Requiem was that it was so vague, especially if all you had for a frame of reference was the corebooks (and for a very long time, that's all I did have). You could make it your own game, it was a toolkit and a sandbox that you could control and were encouraged to do so, unlike the metaplot-driven Masquerade. 2e seems to lack that sort of magic to it, at least for me. And of course, there are the mechanical changes like Touchstones, which is probably the biggest issue I have with 2e.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks for answering.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3326-an-fhuiseog","name":"An Fhuiseog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3326_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"271"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-13T14:58:39-07:00","id":968756,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MercSet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/966373-1e-what-was-playing-vtr-like-in-its-early-days?p=967551#post967551\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Fair dues good sir. I just like using it as a technical term. Because using believability / plausibility and attaching that to a game of make believe monsters, is just hard to justify. Just slipping some academia into a rant.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> My ignorant behind was like &quot;verisimilitude? ain't that academic talk for 'truthiness'?&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5106-umoja","name":"Umoja","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"31"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-13T15:17:12-07:00","id":968775,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Umoja</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/966373-1e-what-was-playing-vtr-like-in-its-early-days?p=968756#post968756\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> My ignorant behind was like &quot;verisimilitude? ain't that academic talk for 'truthiness'?&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>haha, Pretty much that's what it means. Academic term used in novels and playwrights noun 1. the appearance or semblance of truth; likelihood; probability: The play lacked verisimilitude.<br /> <br /> I mostly use it as a scale of how consistent a piece of work plays by its own rules. and I also use it for how closely the piece of work adheres to modern reality. and basically How Believable a setting piece or plot is.<br /> <br /> &quot;Ok given vampires are real and secrete how likely is the mayor to be a vamp out in the open about it?&quot; A high verisimilitude says No, but he may be pawn of the vampire. Or one step better; No but one of his aids and advisors is a blood ghoul who will push for your projects<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Placeholder","edited":{"userid":11731,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11731-mercset","user":"MercSet","date":". 09-13-2016, 06:24 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11731-mercset","name":"MercSet","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11731_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"277"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-13T20:35:48-07:00","id":968963,"title":"","content":"I have to say, 2e fixed almost everything that I didn't like about 1e: when I saw the title of this topic, my immediate mental response was &quot;everyone was super squishy&quot;. Vampires had a lot of powers that were either weak or useless, and a few that were absurdly unbalanced and overpowered. <br /> <br /> The one thing 1e had that 2e didn't was all the bloodlines, and examples in the corebook. But we did get some 2e bloodlines and an outline of the design philosophy for them...and they were awesome, way better than 1e. So bloodlines are even better, I just wish there were more of them. I'd give anything for a B+S Bloodlines book.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5358-geckopirateship","name":"Geckopirateship","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5358_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1383"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-13T20:47:23-07:00","id":968970,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Geckopirateship</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/966373-1e-what-was-playing-vtr-like-in-its-early-days?p=968963#post968963\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have to say, 2e fixed almost everything that I didn't like about 1e: when I saw the title of this topic, my immediate mental response was &quot;everyone was super squishy&quot;. Vampires had a lot of powers that were either weak or useless, and a few that were absurdly unbalanced and overpowered.<br /> <br /> The one thing 1e had that 2e didn't was all the bloodlines, and examples in the corebook. But we did get some 2e bloodlines and an outline of the design philosophy for them...and they were awesome, way better than 1e. So bloodlines are even better, I just wish there were more of them. I'd give anything for a B+S Bloodlines book.</div> </div> </div> </div>I believe we're seeing at least one updated Bloodline in the Dark Eras Companion.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-13T20:53:24-07:00","id":968974,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/966373-1e-what-was-playing-vtr-like-in-its-early-days?p=968970#post968970\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I believe we're seeing at least one updated Bloodline in the Dark Eras Companion.</div> </div> </div> </div>I would give a kidney for a full bloodlines book for 2e, though. 2e Khaibit instantly became one of my favorite things in the setting when I read their writeup.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5358-geckopirateship","name":"Geckopirateship","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5358_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1383"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-13T21:32:23-07:00","id":969001,"title":"","content":"I started playing Masquerade in November of 2004. The next month my father, meaning well, got me &quot;that vampire game&quot; for Christmas. Unwrapping the Requiem book and looking through it, having literally no context, was incredibly confusing. I was only barely aware that other WoD games existed, let alone that they had just ended and were being reimagined in new game lines. <br /> It had kind of uncanny valley quality to it. Just similar enough to what I knew to be recognizable, but different and strange in others that didn't seem to fit. This was pretty much the same response from most of my gaming group, and it was difficult to find people to try it. The D&amp;D crowd took one look at it and decided it was even more pretentious than the old vampire (all the red cursive font didn't really help its case), refusing to play it on principle. The Masquerade crowd was lukewarm on it for all the reasons that have been argued over here a thousand times now. Though I was frustrated by their reaction I will say in their defense that it's intended toolkit approach didn't totally come across, making it feel like it was being deliberately obtuse and vague. Once I got people down for it though the real challenge began. We ditched Predators Taint after two games and never looked back (I still don't really understand how it works). We had a hard time figuring out how the Covenants were supposed to relate to each other and reflexively trying to cram all of them into a city tended to dilute strong themes. Then we were TPK'd by a couple mortals with guns and the whole experiment almost ended there. <br /> Not everyone warmed to it, but some of us did. We came to appreciate the mechanical innovations it contained. We figured out the best way to use Covenants in a story. We figured out that you weren't supposed to start with zero XP anymore. We also figured out that the setting's myriad gray areas were a feature to be exploited. <br /> Honestly more than anything else what prevented it from catching on in a big way with my group was the long delay in releasing LARP materials for it. Most of the gaming in my town was anchored in a good sized local LARP, and despite reservations about it a lot of people were interested to give it a try in that context. By the time the MET book was actually released though pretty much all the momentum was gone and interest had waned.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","name":"Caitiff Primogen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2507_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-13T21:42:24-07:00","id":969003,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Umoja</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/966373-1e-what-was-playing-vtr-like-in-its-early-days?p=968756#post968756\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> My ignorant behind was like &quot;verisimilitude? ain't that academic talk for 'truthiness'?&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>In RPG parlance, it's often used as a stand-in for &quot;realism&quot; in settings that are inherently somewhat unrealistic and/or fantastical.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-13T22:18:03-07:00","id":969023,"title":"","content":"I quit playing white wolf games after a few years because the system was so clunky.<br /> <br /> Requiem brought me back.<br /> It was much more Streamlined for rolls to keep the story moving along. <br /> <br /> I personally didn't mind that the base game felt more like a toolbox than metaplot with bindings. I know that bothered some people. <br /> <br /> I think the only problem I had with 1e was the &quot;monsters&quot; didn't feel very monstrous. Thematically 2e feels more right to me.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1117-overload","name":"overload","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1117_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-13T23:40:30-07:00","id":969048,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/966373-1e-what-was-playing-vtr-like-in-its-early-days?p=969003#post969003\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In RPG parlance, it's often used as a stand-in for &quot;realism&quot; in settings that are inherently somewhat unrealistic and/or fantastical.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes thank you Char. That is a much better explanation of the term and use, than my own <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Placeholder","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11731-mercset","name":"MercSet","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11731_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"277"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}